Login "blinks and stays" with no error (correct password) -- handoff patch
Files: lib/supabase/server.ts, lib/supabase/middleware.ts
(re-cloned fresh from https://github.com/Evolvededen/niore-platform -- confirmed
current, includes the recent "login" / "pass reset supa update" / "update
postgres" commits already)

SYMPTOM REPORTED: typing the correct password causes the page to blink and sit
there with no error message. A wrong password DOES show an error correctly.

WHAT'S CONFIRMED WORKING (ruled out):
- app/(auth)/login/page.tsx's handleLogin() POSTs to /api/auth/signin, which
  is the direct-Postgres path (verifyPassword against auth.users, custom JWT,
  cookies set directly in the response) -- no live Supabase Auth API call here
  at all. This is why a WRONG password correctly errors fast: verifyPassword()
  returns null immediately, no network dependency. Confirmed no bug here.
- On success, the page does router.push('/dashboard') -- straightforward,
  no bug in the client code itself.
- app/dashboard/layout.tsx's own "plan guard" redirect logic (onboarding/
  pricing redirect for non-admin, non-paid accounts) is a fast, synchronous
  redirect() call -- not something that would hang or blink silently.

ROOT CAUSE, CONFIRMED BY CODE STRUCTURE (best explanation available without
runtime/server log access, which this session doesn't have):
Both lib/supabase/server.ts's createClient() and lib/supabase/middleware.ts's
createClient() wrap supabase.auth.getUser() like this:

    const originalGetUser = supabase.auth.getUser.bind(supabase.auth)
    supabase.auth.getUser = async (jwt?) => {
      const result = await originalGetUser(jwt)   // <-- ALWAYS tried first
      if (!result.error) return result
      // ...falls back to local JWT verification only if the above errored
    }

That first call is a real network request to NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL's Auth
API -- the exact API this codebase's own comments describe elsewhere as
broken/bypassed everywhere else (signin, signup, password reset all
explicitly route around it). The fallback only helps if that call FAILS FAST
with an error. If it instead hangs -- unreachable endpoint, DNS not resolving
from inside the Docker container, a firewalled self-hosted Supabase host, etc.
-- there's no timeout anywhere in this code, so the request just sits there
indefinitely with nothing ever throwing. That matches "blinks and stays, no
error" exactly: the login POST already succeeded and set real cookies, but the
very next page (dashboard, which requires this same getUser() check) never
resolves.

This can't be 100% confirmed without server-side network/runtime logs (outside
this session's access), but it fits every piece of evidence: the working
direct-Postgres calls that don't hit this API succeed instantly either way
(correct password: signs in; wrong password: errors fast); only the
GoTrue-dependent path hangs.

RECOMMENDED FIX (requires push access this session does not have):
Stop attempting the live API call at all -- this codebase has already proven
(via signin/signup/password-reset) that the local-JWT-verification path is the
reliable one. In both lib/supabase/server.ts's wrapGetUser() and
lib/supabase/middleware.ts's wrapGetUser(), replace the "try live API first,
fall back to local" order with local-only verification:

    supabase.auth.getUser = async (jwt?: string) => {
      const token = jwt || extractAuthTokenFromCookies(cookieStore)  // (or
                                                                       // extractTokenFromRequest
                                                                       // in middleware.ts)
      if (token) {
        const payload = verifyToken(token)
        if (payload) {
          return { data: { user: { /* same mapping already in the code */ } }, error: null }
        }
      }
      // Only fall back to the live API call if there's no local token at all
      // (e.g. truly logged out) -- and wrap it with a short timeout so it
      // can never hang the page again:
      return await originalGetUser(jwt)
    }

This removes the hang risk entirely for anyone with a valid session cookie
(the common case), while keeping the live-API call only as a last resort for
edge cases with no cookie present at all.

SEPARATE, LOWER PRIORITY: if you want the live API call kept as the
first-choice path for any reason, at minimum wrap it in an AbortController
with a 2-3 second timeout so it fails fast into the fallback instead of
hanging indefinitely. The reorder above is the simpler, more consistent fix
given how the rest of this codebase already treats that API as unreliable.

ALSO WORTH CHECKING (infra-side, not code): confirm NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL
in the Coolify/Docker deployment's environment actually resolves and is
reachable FROM INSIDE the container the Next.js app runs in -- if the
self-hosted migration is partway done, it's possible this points at a host
that's fine from your browser/laptop but not reachable from the container's
network, which would produce exactly this hang. This session has no access to
check the running container's environment or network directly.
